#import "../design.typ": *

#chapter(number: 9, title: "Inner Socrates — the Dialectician")

Everything so far in this book *answers*. The Facts bible answers whether a claim is
true; SENTINEL answers whether the continuity holds; KEN answers whether a character
could know a thing. The four readers in this last part do the opposite, and it is the
harder discipline: they *ask*. They are the *inner family* — a small company of
readers who live inside your manuscript and question it the way a demanding friend
would, each in a different register. The first, and the purest, is Inner Socrates.

#term("The inner readers")[
  Inner Socrates, the Inner Editor, the Inner Theologian, and the Inner Poet are the
  *inner family* — persona readers who examine your prose rather than measure it. They
  never rewrite; they observe, question, and leave the judgement to you. Each keeps its
  own quiet store and surfaces to the Output pane.
]

#section("The one who only asks")

Inner Socrates is a *questioner*, and almost nothing else. It does not praise; it does
not prescribe; it does not tell you what to fix. It reads a passage and asks the
question the prose has closed over — the assumption you never stated, the significance
you have not earned, the objection you did not answer. The point is not the answer.
The point is that a good question, read back at the right moment, does something no
verdict can: it makes you see your own page as a stranger would.

#screen(caption: "Ctrl+B J — a passage, questioned")[```
Inner Socrates · The First-Time Reader
  ◇ You call the treaty "infamous" — infamous to whom, and have we
    been shown why, or only told the word?
  ◇ Two characters agree at once. What would this scene lose if one
    of them resisted first?
```]

#section("A reader for every kind of book")

The genius of Inner Socrates is that "the right question" depends on who is reading.
So it ships a *roster* of more than a dozen personas — you activate the one whose eyes
you want:

#screen(caption: "Ctrl+B J → S cycles the active persona")[```
  fiction     Inner Socrates · Careful Editor · Skeptical Reader ·
              First-Time Reader · Slow Reader
  nonfiction  Skeptical Practitioner · Domain Newcomer ·
              Expert Reviewer · End User
  ideas       The Dialectician · Theological Reader · Utopian Architect
```]

The nonfiction personas stop hunting for scene-time and start asking what a step
assumes the reader already knows. The Dialectician reads philosophy for the unnamed
premise. The Theological Reader is deliberately *non-empiricist* — it probes coherence
and fidelity within a tradition rather than demanding proof. Each is one activation
away, and the fast chord follows the one you chose.

#callout(label: "The two adversaries")[
  Inner Socrates is *always* a neutral questioner — except for two personas that break
  the spine on purpose. The *Defender* states only what works and why to protect it;
  the *Prosecutor* states only what fails, as a charge. When you want an argument out
  loud rather than a question, these are it.
]

#section("Fast and slow")

`Ctrl+B J` opens the overview — the active persona, recent questions, the ledger of
what you have already considered. The *fast* track is deterministic and instant. When
you want the deeper reading, `Ctrl+B J → E` engages the *slow* track — a cost-capped
language pass that reads the passage in the active persona's voice. Notice, Inquiry,
Probe: the questions rank from a surface observation to a structural challenge to the
whole work, and the quiet default shows you only the ones worth stopping for.

